    id: "ecfb6a78ff7765b9d853",
    cat: "conflitti",
    title: "Netanyahu demands probe into sources for award-winning Gaza film NAZA",
    summary: "Documentary draws on 24 anonymous Israeli sources accusing military of killing Palestinians in Gaza using AI systems.",
    preview: "Documentary draws on 24 anonymous Israeli sources accusing military of killing Palestinians in Gaza using AI systems. Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu has called for an investigation into the anonymous sources featured in a documentary alleging that Israel’s military killed civilians in Gaza using AI-powered targeting systems. The award-winning film, NAZA, draws on testimony from 24 unnamed Israeli military whistleblowers accusing the army of using the systems to kill numerous civilians in the besieged territory.",
    body: "Documentary draws on 24 anonymous Israeli sources accusing military of killing Palestinians in Gaza using AI systems.\n\nIsraeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu has called for an investigation into the anonymous sources featured in a documentary alleging that Israel’s military killed civilians in Gaza using AI-powered targeting systems.\n\nThe award-winning film, NAZA, draws on testimony from 24 unnamed Israeli military whistleblowers accusing the army of using the systems to kill numerous civilians in the besieged territory.\n\nRecommended Stories\n\nlist of 3 items\n\n- list 1 of 3Child among eight killed by Israel on Gaza ‘ceasefire’ anniversary\n\n- list 2 of 3Hundreds of doctors demand World Medical Association suspend Israel\n\n- list 3 of 3How genocidal war on Gaza is shaping Israel’s election\n\n“I demand that the chief of general staff, the head of the Shin Bet, and the attorney general launch a comprehensive investigation into the participants in the film ‘Naza’ and prosecute them for serious information security violations, criminal libel, and harm to Israel’s diplomatic and security interests,” Netanyahu said on X on Saturday evening.\n\n“The film presents a false, misleading, and biased narrative that defames the State of Israel and the [Israeli military] internationally, severely damaging Israel’s standing,” he added.\n\nIn a statement last month, the Israeli military said it “categorically” rejected the whistleblowers’ allegations, calling some of their assertions “absurd’ and contradictory.\n\nIt insisted that the selection and approval of strikes “are made by human personnel only”, in line with the military’s guidelines.\n\nIsraeli army Chief of Staff Eyal Zamir has called for legal action against the participants to be considered and ordered an investigation into suspected leaks of classified military information used in the film’s production.\n\nNAZA takes its name from the Israeli military term for civilian casualties expected in air strikes, an abbreviated form of the Hebrew phrase for “collateral damage”.\n\nThe film was awarded a special jury prize at the Venice International Film Festival and received a historic 25-minute standing ovation.\n\nIt has also triggered a political storm in Israel.\n\nThe film’s directors, Yuval Abraham and Rachel Szor, have been accused of “treason”. Netanyahu and Culture Minister Miki Zohar have threatened to revoke their Israeli citizenship.\n\nLast week, the head of Israel’s National Public Diplomacy Directorate, Tzipi Hotovely, awarded the creators of another film, NAZA: The Real Story, certificates of recognition for countering the “blood libels” made in the original NAZA film.\n\nAccording to a statement from the Prime Minister’s Office, NAZA: The Real Story features testimony from soldiers refuting the original claims.\n\nIn contrast, 43 Israeli rights groups defended the filmmakers of NAZA and denounced the threats being directed at them, according to Israel’s Channel 12 news.\n\nIsrael’s genocidal war on Gaza began on October 7, 2023, after Hamas’s armed wing, the Qassam Brigades, and other Palestinian groups attacked southern Israel, killing 1,139 people and taking about 240 captive.\n\nSince then, Israel has killed at least 74,000 Palestinians, including at least 21,000 children, according to Gaza’s Ministry of Health.",
